// The compiled producer Mind, transported from the Mac stack (auto-generated).
export const MIND: string = "# MIND: The Producer's Working Memory (single-file boot brain)\n\nGrids: 1 bar = 16 steps `1e&a 2e&a 3e&a 4e&a`. `X` accent (110\u2013127), `x` normal (75\u2013100), `g` ghost (35\u201360), `O` open hat, `.` rest. Clap 2+4 = steps 5+13. Swing = MPC convention (50% straight, 66.7% triplet), on 2nd+4th 16ths. Bass symbols: R root, 5, b7, b3, O octave up, L leading tone, s slide. Devices = Live 12 Intro stock. \u2248 = estimate.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE LAWS\n\n1. **GROOVE** = medium syncopation in the LOW register + loudness breathing at beat rate + systematic (never random) micro-timing. 2\u20134 TRUE syncopations per 2-bar loop: an event on a weak position with the next strong position EMPTIED; all-offbeat-8ths is a second grid, not syncopation. RMS variability at beat rate is the #1 groove predictor (r=.57): pump, accent waves, ghosts (vel 20\u201350). Random jitter HURTS groove. Humanize = FINGERPRINT: one per-slot deviation map repeated every bar \u00b12 ms + systematic pocket (clap +8\u201315 ms late, bass +15, melody 20\u201340 EARLY, kick dead on grid) + identical 16th swing 54\u201358% every bar. Keep one unsyncopated sacred timekeeper (4-floor kick + shaker); bright offbeat = the \"up\" cue.\n2. **BASS CARRIES IDENTITY**: in ~2/3 of canon grooves the bassline is the melodic signature (root\u2192b7\u21925 walks, riffs with rests, octave bounce, ghosts, slides). Stabs CONVERSE with the bass, never double its rhythm.\n3. **REPETITION IS THE ENGINE, VIOLATION THE PAYOFF**: 1\u20132 bar cells looped with discipline; mutate every 4\u20138 bars; engineered stops/dropouts/unisons/fills are the cheapest dopamine: one every 4\u20138 bars. Minimum 3 one-off ear-candy moments per song; zero one-offs = sounds like a preset.\n4. **CONTRAST MAKES DROPS**: thin the last bar before; silence gap; impact stack on the One (crash + sub-drop + full entry); ONE new element 8 bars in; drop 2 = drop 1 + exactly one layer.\n5. **HOOKS**: small intervals, arch contour, long notes on rhyme anchors, ONE twist, stated 3\u00d7 minimum, inside 7 seconds, and INSIDE the drop: the clippable 15 s must contain the words.\n6. **SOUNDS ARE HISTORY**: choose them for what they SAY: 909 = house's flag (offbeat OH); 808 = trap sub; 303 = acid scream; Juno = deep warmth; M1 Organ 2 = 90s house bass-hook, M1 Piano = rave euphoria; LinnDrum = MTV sheen; DX7 = digital 80s; filtered disco loop = French school; log drum = amapiano; Moroder sequenced 8ths = every machine bass since. Crossover invariant: what survives underground\u2192pop = the groove engine + ONE signature timbre; what gets traded = song form, vocal polish, harmonic simplification. Protect the timbre and floor pattern; negotiate everything else. Revival cycles run ~20\u201330 years, mechanically.\n7. **REGISTER LAW**: never write a sampler melody more than \u00b15 st from the sample's root unless chipmunk is the explicit goal (Brady scar tissue: the \"screech\" was a C4 sample played +16 st).\n8. **KEYS/TEMPO**: house 120\u2013128 minor (A minor most common; ~65% of dance charts minor); pop follows the singer's range; 2 Hz body resonance means 120\u2013130 owns the floor. Modern hits split both ways (barbell: see \u00a75).\n9. **MIX DOCTRINE**: kick \u2248\u22128 dBFS reference; ONE rival to the kick at a time (the hook); sidechain release \u226460% of beat interval; mono <100\u2013120 Hz; high-pass everything that isn't bass; dry-forward vocals.\n10. **FAMILIARITY BEATS OPTIMIZATION** (MAYA): style-typical sounds rate higher than clever ones; songs moderately LESS typical than their field beat both extremes. Innovate on ONE axis per track; no canon record does two tricks.\n11. **SAMPLING LAW**: public-domain (pre-1923/CC0) and licensed sources only; never rip commercial recordings. Familiarity is a legal concept-space, not a copying license.\n\n**Song anatomy**: track each PRESENT/WEAK/MISSING: pulse; low end owning 40\u2013100 Hz; harmony bed; THE HOOK stated ~3\u00d7; a signature sound (the fingerprint); one-offs \u22653; tension/release with \u22651 breakdown; transitions (nothing teleports); space (something expected removed); a first-7-seconds reason to care; an actual ending. Open every session by naming the weakest item.\n\n**Feel-word\u2192move grammar**: \"more groove\"\u2192+1 syncopation in bass, swing to 56%, ghosts up, check stab/bass redundancy. \"Too rigid\"\u2192NOT random jitter: syncopation first, dynamics second, swing third, arrangement variation fourth. \"More energy/bigger\"\u2192density vs 5-element drop, sidechain depth, open-hat presence, unison moment. \"Cheap/kidsey\"\u2192SAMPLE REGISTER vs written range FIRST, then quantization, then preset quality. \"Doesn't hit\"\u2192pre-drop contrast, impact stack, 40\u2013100 Hz weight. Before any sampler melody: confirm the root.\n\n---\n\n## 2. GROOVE NUMBERS\n\n**Swing %\u2192ms** (delay on every 2nd/4th 16th; 16th = 125 ms @120, 121 @124, 117 @128). At 124: 52% = +4.8 ms barely there (prog/French); 54% = +9.7 bounce creeps in (house starts 54\u201357); 56% = +14.5 deep house pocket; 58% = +19.4 classic house shuffle; 60% = +24.2 rolling deep; 62% = +29.0 garage lean; 66.7% = +40.3 full triplet, jackin' extreme.\n\nGroove file per subgenre (HATS+PERC only; kick and clap committed, except jackin' where clap swings too): Chicago none (ghost kicks do the work); Deep 57\u201360 + Random 4\u20136% hats; Tech 55\u201358 (shaker grooved, ride not); Jackin' MPC-62\u201366 incl. clap; French none (the loop carries feel); Bass house 52\u201354 @80%; Afro 55 + manual \u00b110\u201320 ms conga nudges; Amapiano 52\u201355, log hand-placed. Groove Pool: Base 1/16, Timing 100%, Random 0% (hats 4\u20138%); commit before final bounce. ONE groove file per project: a single straight shaker can kill a swung groove. Track Delay: kick 0 (never moves); clap +4 to +12 ms (deep pocket) OR \u22123 to \u22125 (jack/definition: one school per track); shaker \u22123 to \u22125; congas \u00b110\u201320 manual; log composed, not offset. Humanize ceiling \u00b18 ms / \u00b18 vel, hats+perc only. If kick or clap ever moves, it stops being house.\n\n**Velocity conventions**: kick 115\u2013127 constant (never humanize kick velocity), clap 110\u2013120, OH 85\u2013105, CH accents 90\u2013110 / ghosts 35\u201360, shaker 55\u201395 shaped, perc 60\u201390, toms 70\u2013100. Randomize hats \u00b15\u20138 max; kick/clap \u00b10.\n\n**Hat accent shapes** (16th velocities `1 e & a`): offbeat push 70/50/110/55; flat carpet 65\u00d74 +5% random; a-gallop 90/40/60/100 (jackin'); e-skip 80/\u2013/95/60 (garage); crescendo 55/70/85/100 (tech shaker). Hat accents the \"&\", shaker the \"a\" = push-pull, pan \u00b110\u201320. Ghost hats 35\u201350 flanking accents; drop one hat's velocity at step 10 for shuffle.\n\n**Pocket catalog**: MACHINE (house/EDM): kick/clap/bass dead on grid, all feel from swing + clap nudge (quantized is CORRECT here; exaggerated looseness always loses). ON-TOP (disco/funk): hats/skank 3\u201310 ms early. BEHIND (neo-soul): drums hold grid, band trails 20\u201380 ms. DILLA: kick 10\u201330 early, snare 15\u201340 late, hats straight, CONSISTENT. KAYTRANADA (reverse polarity): kick on grid, hats slightly LATE, snare slightly EARLY, constant. Rule: the layer the listener tracks for time stays most stable; offsets go around it.\n\n**Syncopation cells** (steps): BACKBEAT 5,13; OFF-8s 3,7,11,15; CHARLESTON 1+7; TRESILLO 1,7,13 (3+3+2: the defining pop rhythm; standard EDM stab rhythm); HABANERA tresillo+9; CINQUILLO 1,5,7,11,13; DOUBLE TRESILLO 1,4,7,10,13,15; SON CLAVE 3-2 = bar1 tresillo, bar2 beats 2+3 (built-in tension/resolve); DEMBOW 4-floor kick + snares 4,7,12,15; AMAPIANO LOG: avoid step 1 of bar 1, 16th pairs landing INTO beats, \u22651 sustained note per 2 bars, call bar 1/answer bar 2, log = bassline AND sole syncopator. Cross-rhythm safe only when: pulse intact and loud, cycle resolves bar 4/8, ONE polyrhythmic layer, timbrally separated (forms: dotted-8th ostinato, dotted-quarter 3-bar loop, 3+3+2 regroup, triplet burst).\n\n**Half-time switch**: backbeat 2+4\u2192beat 3 only, halve kick density, KEEP the hi-hat 16th rate: tactus halves, surface energy unchanged, reads \"heavier\"; re-entrainment in 1\u20132 bars. Change the TACTUS, never the grid. **Tempo\u2192body map**: 96\u2013110 hips/dembow; 108 hip-sway; 112\u2013116 amapiano lope; 118\u2013128 house 2-step (2 Hz sweet spot); 135\u2013150 only via half-time tactus; 165\u2013175 double-time over ~85 sway. Felt beat at 1.7\u20132.1 Hz.\n\n**Bass groove numbers**: 16th swing 55\u201358% on basslines (Swing 16 groove, 20\u201340% amount); Dolla shuffle = swing on the bass channel, drums straighter. Late = fat: 5\u201315 ms late reads laid-back; dead-on = machine drive; NEVER early (flams the kick). Ghosts vel 30\u201340% on the \"a\" before downbeats, one per bar. Velocity: offbeats 96\u2013110, gallop partners 70\u201385, ghosts 35\u201345; velocity\u2192cutoff +20\u201330% so accents are spectral. Note length is groove: gates 90%\u219255% of the 8th = pump with zero sidechain; combine both for the modern sound.\n\n**Construction order**: body target\u2192tempo/tactus; anchor (4-floor / backbeat / half-time / sparse); subdivider rate + ONE swing value; ONE syncopator cell with its own register+timbre; pocket fingerprint; tension schedule 4/8/16-bar. Four roles: TIMEKEEPER (loudest, stable) / SUBDIVIDER (owns the swing) / SYNCOPATOR (the identity: one at a time; a tresillo marimba counts, so drums stay plain) / COLORIST (never loops; density rises toward section ends). Rhythm rubric (0\u20132 each, ship \u226516/20): pulse clarity; syncopation budget; swing coherence; role coverage; pocket fingerprint; density arc; low-end alignment; hook ownership; body-tempo fit; \u22651 sectional rhythm event.\n\n**Pump numbers** (bass, keyed from kick): subtle glue 2\u20134:1, rel 40\u201380, 2\u20134 dB; standard tech 4:1, rel 60\u2013120, 4\u20136 dB; audible pump 6:1+, rel 100\u2013180, 8\u201312 dB; attack 0.1\u20131 ms. Release recovers before the next offbeat bass note (240 ms after kick @125\u2192rel \u2264~180); general law: rel \u226460% of beat interval (\u2248290 ms @124). Duck the SUB hard (6\u201312 dB), the MID lightly or not at all. Deterministic alternative: LFO\u2192Utility gain, 1/4 saw-down.\n\n---\n\n## 3. DRUM + BASS GRAMMARS\n\n### 3.1 Drum subgenre table\n\n| Subgenre | BPM | Swing | Kick | Backbeat | Signature |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chicago | 118\u2013124 | 50\u201353 | 4/4 + ghost \"a of 4\" | 909/707 clap | jack from ghost kicks; zero humanization authentic |\n| Deep | 118\u2013124 | 56\u201362 (rolling 60\u201370) | 4/4 | clap +5\u201310 ms late | rim answers, toms 3/9/11, shaker carpet |\n| Tech | 124\u2013128 | 55\u201360 hats/perc ONLY | 4/4 straight | short pitched clap+snare | kick/clap on grid; OH once per 2\u20134 bars |\n| Jackin'/garage | 120\u2013126 | 62\u201370+ | 4/4 + offbeats bar 2 | 4th clap pulled EARLY | heaviest shuffle |\n| French | 122\u2013127 | 50\u201352 | 4/4 loud, king | loop snare + 909 clap | filtered disco loop, hard sidechain |\n| Bass house | 126\u2013128 | 50\u201354 | 4/4 short | big layered clap | offbeat BASS replaces the OH; drop = kick+bass+clap |\n| Afro | 118\u2013124 | 54\u201358 + hand offsets | 4/4 round | snap or rim | conga tumbao, 3-2 clave (1,4,7,11,13), endless shaker |\n| Amapiano | 112\u2013116 | 50\u201356 | sparse till peak | sparse woodblock | log drum = melodic bass hook |\n| Prog | 120\u2013126 | 50\u201352 | 4/4 | rim/soft or none | low-vel 8th hats/ride; motion from synths |\n| Minimal | 123\u2013127 | 55\u201360 | 4/4 | mid snare+noise clap | glitch one-shots mirror the hats |\n\nCore grids (kick `X... X... X... X...`, clap steps 5+13, OH steps 3,7,11,15 assumed):\n```\nDEEP   CH x.g. xgg. x.g. xg.g   SHKR gxgx gxgX...  TOM ..x. .... x.x. ....\nTECH   CH ..X. ..X. ..X. ..X.   PERC .x.. ...x .x.. ...x   GS ..g. before 2/4\nBASSH  BASS ..X. ..X. ..X. ..X. (the bass IS the hat)  CH quiet sparse\nAFRO   SHKR xxxx\u2026 loose  CGA ..o. s... ..o. .o.s  CLV X..X ..X. ..X. X...\nAMA    K .... .... ..X. ....  LOG X..X ..X. .X.. X.X. (pitched R/5)  SHKR never stops\n```\nOH: decay 300\u2013500 ms deep / 150\u2013250 tech; OH+CH in one choke group, always. Clap by era: Chicago bare on-grid\u2192garage clap+noise snare, big hall, 4th clap early\u21922010s deep clap+rim +5\u201310 late, plate 0.8\u20131.2 s\u2192tech \"cardboard\" clap few ms early\u21922020s 3-layer stack tuned to key. Layer recipe: snare body + mono clap transient + wide clap; offset each \u22125..+5 ms; HPF layers 2\u20133 at 120 Hz. Perc lives around the backbeat on offbeats; never grid-quantize hand percussion.\n\n**Fills & transitions: frequency rule**: 4 bars = micro-variation; 8 = one-beat fill; 16 = one-bar fill or cut; 32 = arrangement change + crash. Vocabulary: clap roll (steps 9,11,13\u201316, vel 70\u2192127); snare rush (16ths 60\u2192127, last bar); tom run 11,13,15; THE CUT (mute kick or all, steps 13\u201316: house's #1 transition); open-hat ring step 15; reverse crash/vocal; kick drag 16,1; filter sweep HPF 20\u2192800 Hz over 4 bars, snap on 1 (full-bus sweeps 4\u201316 bars = French grammar); downlifter; tape stop; stutter/beat-mash last bar; impact + falling sine on the drop downbeat; kick removal (softest signpost); vocal call last 2 bars. Modern 8-bar build: bars 1\u20134 snare 8ths + riser; 5\u20136 16ths ramping; 7 32nds; 8 = SILENCE beats 3\u20134 + shout; land crash + full pattern + bass. Withhold shaker/16th hats for the drop's first 8 bars, add at bar 9. DJ grammar: 16\u201332 bars kick+hats intro/outro; crash on the 1 of every 32.\n\n**Kick numbers**: fundamental 45\u201355 Hz, tuned to root or fifth (F1 43.7 / G1 49.0 / A1 55.0 Hz; C1\u2013D1 roots are felt not heard: use the fifth). 909 sampler env: atk 0, dec \u2248267 ms, sus \u221231 dB, rel \u2248414. Tech kick 200\u2013300 ms total. EQ: cut \u2248350 box \u22122 to \u22124; click 2\u20134 kHz (up for tech, down for chug). Comp 4:1, 10\u201330/50\u2013100 ms, 2\u20134 dB; parallel slam 10:1 blend 20\u201330%. Drum bus glue 4:1, 30 ms, auto, 2\u20134 dB + light sat. Era law: 909-one-source vs modern 2\u20133-layer (sub+punch+click, \u00d73 claps, drier): never mix aesthetics in one kit.\n\n### 3.2 Bass lineage grammars\n\n| Lineage | Grammar |\n|---|---|\n| **M1 organ** (Show Me Love) | C2\u2013C3, riff = hook, answers vocal. `R..R ..O. R... b7.R.`: root-centric 2-bar riff, 16th pickups, octave pop on offbeat 8th, b7 turnaround; gate 60\u2013120 ms; needs a sub layer under (thin <80 Hz) |\n| **Rolling tech** (FISHER/Lake/Dolla) | never lands on the kick. Core `..R. ..R. ..R. ..RR`; gallop `..RR ..RR ..RR ..Rb7`. Offbeats only, gate 50\u201370% of gap, vel 100/80, vel\u2192filter, ONE pitch move (b7/b6/b2) at step 16 of bar 2/4; slight swing mandatory |\n| **Disco octave** (PDM/AVH) | `R.O. R.O. R.O. R.L.`: root rings, octave short; pentatonic + leading tone on the \"a\" before each change (removing the L kills it) |\n| **Deep subby** (Heard) | sine/soft square E1\u2013A1: sustained roots at chord changes, OR 8th arp R-5-O-5 per beat-pair |\n| **Acid 303** | 16th stream, 1\u20133 pitches + octave jumps, 30\u201370% filled; slides (overlaps, glide 60\u201390 ms) + accents (vel 127) are the whole language; 6/7/9-note cycles vs the 16 grid; automate cutoff over 8\u201316 bars, res high |\n| **Bass house wobble** | LFO on cutoff synced 1/8 (1/4 = menace), riff 100\u2013800 Hz, static sine sub under; 2-bar call, bar 3 repeat, bar 4 response |\n| **Afro/log** | tuned sub IS the melody: sine + woody transient, E1\u2013E2, 3\u20135 hits/bar, `R... ...b3 ..5. .R..`, downward bends, deep swing, lighter kick |\n\n**Kick\u2013bass**: split every non-sine bass: SUB mono LP 80\u2013100 Hz, ducked hard, no stereo; MID HP 100\u2013180 Hz carries saturation/width, ducked lightly; a gap (LP 80\u201390 / HP 150) buys kick punch room. Decide the floor: kick-low/bass-above (tech standard, kick ~50 Hz) or bass-low/kick-above (deep, afro); cut 2\u20134 dB in each where the other's fundamental sits.\n\n**Bass writing rules**: bass carries melody only when nothing else does: vocal enters, bass collapses to root pedal + rhythm. Call-and-response with vocals: movement in the gaps, root under sung notes. One pitch change per bar max in rolling styles, at steps 15\u201316. Loops: tech 1\u20132 bars, organ/vocal 2, disco 2\u20134 w/ bar-4 turnaround, deep 4\u20138. Registers: subs E1\u2013A1, character C2\u2013C3; a singable riff needs energy above 200 Hz. Minor pentatonic + leading tones covers 90%; semitone tension loops are legit modern grammar. Drop 1 full pattern; breakdown = sub out; drop 2 earns one variation. Mix: HP the bass 25\u201330 Hz; saturate MID only (2\u20133\u20134\u00d7 harmonics read on phones; sub stays clean sine); phone test = mid saturation, not more sub.\n\n### 3.3 Keys, chords, stabs, vocals\n\n**The m9 is THE deep house chord** (R-b3-5-b7-9): one m9 stabbed reads deep house alone. Voicings: Chicago spread stab G2-C3-E3; piano house LH octaves + RH close triads; m9 rootless (bass has root) slid in parallel; quartal; any triad over a foreign bass note = instant 11th. Octave-displace one chord tone so adjacent chords share close voicings: the top notes form their own melody (harmony and melody are usually the same object in house).\n\n**Progression bank (Am, transpose)**: i\u2013VI\u2013VII Am7\u2192Fmaj7\u2192G (deep default); i\u2013VI\u2013III\u2013VII Am\u2192F\u2192C\u2192G (festival); m9 vamp Am9\u2194Dm9 (hypnotic); i\u2013iv\u2013VI\u2013v (melancholy vocal); Dorian lift Am7\u2192Bm7\u2192Fmaj7\u2192Em7; gospel major C\u2192C/E\u2192F\u2192G7sus4\u2192G7; MAW all-7ths Am7\u2192Cmaj7\u2192Em7; Phrygian dark Am\u2192Bbmaj7; French loop Em7\u2192Fmaj7\u2192Cmaj7\u2192Fmaj7. 2/4-bar loops, voice-lead shared tones, fewer chords + better filter moves; write in Am/Dm/Em, transpose so the sub sits E1\u2013A1.\n\n**Stab rhythms** (short, offbeat bias, space is an element): offbeat pump `..x.` \u00d74; Chicago push `x... x... x... .x..`; dotted-8 chain `x..x ..x. .x.. x...`; deep long stab on offbeats of 1+3; tech sparse: one stab per 1\u20132 bars in synced delay; 2-bar Q/A cell. Alternate vel ~80/100.\n\n**Five vocal traditions**: DIVA BELT (A3\u2013E5; chorus doubled + 3rds stack, plate; duck pads not drums); CHOPPED RIFF (MK school: syllables resequenced into a new melody, no real words; repitch, no formant correction: the artifact IS the sound; \u00b13\u20137 st; end on open vowels; Brady exception: no chipmunk pitch-UPS); SPOKEN/SASSY (deadpan 1\u20132 bar mantra, dry mono up front, phone-EQ HP300/LP4k, stutter the last word); GOSPEL CHOIR (3\u20135-part triads answering lead, enters final drop); FRENCH FILTERED LOOP (sung phrase looped 2 bars as instrument; filter = arrangement; keep grit). House hook: \u22642 bars, 3\u20137 words, must survive 64 repeats; test = mute all else, loop 1 min. Melody: 1\u20132 bar cell, vary every 4th/8th bar; minor pentatonic + 9th/b5; riff inside one octave C4\u2013C6, toplines A3\u2013E5; notes in the gaps, beat 1 sometimes empty; offbeat anchor (all-downbeat = trance); too repetitive soloed = usually right in the mix: change the FILTER, not the notes. Pads: chord tones \u2265C3, HP 150\u2013300, 9th on top, attack 100\u2013500 ms, sidechained.\n\n### 3.4 Synth recipes (Drift/Simpler, compressed)\n\nKick: sine, Env2\u2192pitch +30 st D 40 ms, amp D 320 (tight 150); play the note whose tail lands 40\u201355 Hz; resample to Drum Rack; Sub: sine \u22121 + saw 12%, LP 350, no velocity ever; Rolling bass: saw + square \u22121, LP II 300 res 15\u201325, env +2.5 oct D 150, amp D 350, mono legato, Saturator; vel\u2192env 25%; Acid: saw, LP II 220 res 60\u201375, env +3.5 oct, glide 60\u201390 legato, vel\u2192env = accent; Pluck: 2 saws +10c, env +2.5 oct D 200 S 0, dotted-1/8 delay makes the riff; m9 pad: saw+saw +12c, LP 1 kHz, A 250 S 90 R 900, LFO 0.15 Hz\u2192filter; Organ bass: square + sine \u22121, LP 900, A 1 S 100 R 25 (gate = the bounce), staccato 16ths; FM knock: sine \u22121, Env2\u2192pitch +9 st D 25 ms; Riser: noise, LP 600\u219216 kHz over 4\u20138 bars + vol \u221212\u21920; cut dead ON the downbeat; Hats: noise HP 7 kHz, D 35/300, choked; vel\u2192vol 70%; Vocal chop: Simpler slice; Warp OFF/Re-Pitch = classic sampled character, Complex Pro = modern natural; 3 ms fades; HP 180\u2013250; formants at or below natural (taste ledger). Layers: bass 2 (sub+mid), lead 2\u20133, kick 1\u20132: past 3 = mud; sub always one mono layer <100 Hz. Glide: bass 30\u201360 ms, acid 60\u2013100. Five knobs: cutoff, env decay, detune/spread, send, sample start. Loop-point pad: any sustained sample\u2192Simpler loop 100\u2013300 ms crossfaded + slow attack. Resample your own patches constantly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SOUND COHESION: the 12-point fit check\n\nThe ear fuses or segregates by measurable cues; a newcomer is \"in the record\" in proportion to how many it shares. **Share \u22654 before touching EQ.** Free starter kit: groove + pump + shared reverb + shared saturation. The 8 cues: onset synchrony (<10 ms); attack similarity; spectral centroid (dark fuses, bright segregates: the brighter, the more other cues it must share); formant alignment (differences above ~500 Hz matter most); harmonicity (octaves/5ths fuse; cents-detune beats); common fate (breathes with the pump); spatial location (same room); medium continuity (same noise floor/ceiling/saturation). Slower attacks blend; when percussive + sustained fuse, the sustained one defines the timbre. High register weakens blend, period.\n\nAttack classes: hard <5 ms (drums/plucks/stabs: max ONE hard-attack melodic voice at a time); shaped 5\u201350 ms (keys/bass/hooks); swell >100 ms (carpet only). Bright-pluck-on-warm-bed fails centroid AND attack: fix both (LP to 3\u20136 kHz + attack 10\u201320 ms) or feature it as THE ear-candy \u22643\u00d7: never the half-bright middle. Brightness families: dark <1 kHz, mid 1\u20132.5 kHz, bright >2.5 kHz: the newcomer's centroid lands inside the family it joins.\n\nEra matching: 60s\u201370s tape = hiss \u2248\u221250 dB + LP 10\u201314 kHz + soft sat; 12-bit sampler = Redux 12-bit/26 kHz; 90s DAT = LP \u224815 kHz + bright room; modern ITB = reference. **Match DOWN, never up**; one SHARED Saturator (and Redux if lo-fi) = one tape machine, one story. Palette law: fewest sources = automatic cohesion: can Drift or an existing sound play it? If the novelty budget is spent, the newcomer is genre-fluent and boring on purpose.\n\nTuning: find the root, transpose within \u00b15 st (law 7). Cents: 0\u20135 free (reads as chorus); 6\u201315 sour on sustained: FIX (10 cents beats at ~2.5 Hz); 15\u201350 out to civilians; >50 wrong note. Short decay hides detune; tune drums to root/5th anyway. Slow-beat test: hold against root pad, adjust Detune till the wobble stops. Formant fights: an element peaking in the vocal's 400 Hz\u20131 kHz / 1\u20134 kHz bands = two voices arguing: move an octave or duck 2\u20134 dB at 3\u20135 kHz when the vocal's in.\n\nRhythm: THE SWING CONTRACT: everything playing 8ths/16ths obeys the same swing % (4-floor kick exempt); straight loop into swung groove = two drummers. If the import's pocket is BETTER: Extract Groove, apply to everything, commit: both directions work, splitting doesn't. Transients: <10 ms fuse (check phase); 10\u201330 ms flam, worst zone; >30 ms separate event, better be intended. Density slotting: >70% onset-coincident = layer (lock 100%); <30% = call-and-response (good); 30\u201370% = mud.\n\nMix slots: <60 Hz kick+sub CLOSED; 60\u2013120 bass; 120\u2013250 one guest max; 250\u2013500 THE MUD ZONE, vacancy check mandatory; 500\u20132k main melee; 2\u20135k vocal right-of-way; >5k hats. Three legal moves: claim vacant; evict (cut incumbent 2\u20134 dB); bracket (HP AND LP the newcomer to only its band). Center reserved (kick/bass/lead); only 1\u20132 elements get WIDE. Pump: sustained elements duck to kick with the family release (\u226460% beat, \u00b120% of family); pump what fights the kick, leave true transients alone. Space: 2\u20133 spaces per record TOTAL; newcomers join an EXISTING send, never their own insert verb; depth = send amount + pre-delay (50\u201380 ms upfront, 0\u201320 into the wall).\n\nAudition law: **never shop in solo** (Hot-Swap with the loop running); gain-match to role level first (+1 dB always reads \"better\"); tune/trim/level + shared buses are free: beyond that, more than TWO corrective processors = wrong source, replace (the browser is cheaper than the rescue); right body wrong attack = layer (incumbent attack + newcomer body, aligned <10 ms, HP'd, tuned). In/out test on 8 bars: IN inevitable, OUT missing; OUT = relief\u2192delete; IN unnoticeable\u2192redundant. Confirm tomorrow, small and quiet.\n\n| # | Property | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Key/root | root known; within \u00b15 st of sample root, in key |\n| 2 | Fine tune | sustained \u00b15 cents, short \u00b115; no beating vs root pad |\n| 3 | Tempo/warp | locked to BPM, transients on grid |\n| 4 | Swing | same groove file/% as track (house 54\u201358) |\n| 5 | Attack class | matches role: carpet=swell, hook=shaped/hard |\n| 6 | Brightness | centroid inside the family it joins |\n| 7 | Frequency slot | dominant band vacant or eviction planned |\n| 8 | Stereo slot | assigned position; no wide-on-wide |\n| 9 | Era/texture | noise floor + ceiling match neighbors |\n| 10 | Space | on an existing send; wet/dry matches section |\n| 11 | Pump | sidechain source + release match family (\u226460% beat) |\n| 12 | Role | answers a hole (<30% overlap) or locked layer (100%); mute test passes |\n\nSymptoms: \"two drummers\"\u2192swing (#3\u20134); floats on top\u2192no pump (#11); \"another room/record\"\u2192space or era (#9\u201310); harsh, always in front\u2192too bright + fast for role (#5\u20136); sour wobble\u2192cents (#2); mix got SMALLER\u2192slot war, usually 250\u2013500 (#7); fine but foreign\u2192palette/lineage says the wrong decade. Closing law: the bed defines the physics; the newcomer obeys, or it IS the news: only the track's ONE featured novelty may break the cues.\n\n---\n\n## 5. SONG DNA: 37 records, one line each\n\nUse: set BPM+key first (tempo IS the genre statement). Copy the drop element count/roles literally (\u22646: emulation failures are additions). Copy ONE mechanism per track; the rest stays Brady's. STD-CLUB = \u224816-bar DJ intro / 16 build / 16\u201332 drop / 16 break / 8 build / 32 drop 2 (= drop 1 + one layer) / 16 outro. POP-FORM = verse/pre/chorus-as-drop, radio length.\n\n**Modern tech house**: Turn Off the Lights (Lake '18, 125 Bm): kick+bass fused via velocity\u2192filter accents; whispered 5-word command as texture; I Remember (Lake '20, 126 \u2248E): vocal loop chosen for the FEELING of memory over one ecstatic chord; Take It (Dolla '18, 123 \u2248C): topline = one pitched-down spoken phrase stuttered; swing on shaker/bass, not kick; girl$ (Dolla '24, 134 \u2248Gm): two-stage drop, 16th hats join bar 9; Dreamin (Dolla '24, 134 \u2248G): pop chorus grafted onto peak-time drive; tempo as statement; Losing It (FISHER '18, 125 G): semitone bass riff G\u2194A\u266d IS the song; silence-then-shout gags; extra-long builds; Atmosphere (FISHER '23, 126 \u2248A\u266d): emotional sung chorus saved for the break; drop answers wordlessly, kick+bass+clap; Deep End (Summit '20, 126 C\u266fm): audible pump as identity; 16th &-a gallop, octave jumps at 8-bar turns; Where You Are (Summit & Hayla '23, 126 Am): trance-scale belt over tech kit; low-vel 16th hat carpet; Drugs From Amsterdam (Mau P '22, 125 \u2248E\u266d): ALL character in the talking formant bass 100\u2013800 Hz; everything else deliberately boring; Stephanie (Cloonee '23, 124 Em): flip a known rap hook, arrange AROUND its flow; Push To Start (Noizu/Westend '22, 128 C\u266fm): 3-word command doubling as drop cue; Locco (Biscits '22, 126 Gm): bass call / one-word answer as the entire hook economy; Do It To It (ACRAZE '21, 125 Bm): use the WHOLE flipped a cappella; verses give song shape; Ferrari (Hype '22, 125 C\u266fm): vocal stutter edits AS fills every 8 bars.\n\n**Vocal/piano canon**: Show Me Love (Robin S '93, \u2248122 \u2248Fm): hook lives in the BASS register (M1 Organ 2), vocal answers; snare not clap, huge rolls; Finally (CeCe '91, 120 Bm): title = first word on the downbeat with melisma; ad-libs fill every gap; Gypsy Woman ('91, \u2248120 \u2248Am): scat syllables beat words; continuous 16th shaker defines it; Missing (Todd Terry mix '95, 123 \u2248Am): keep the sad vocal untouched, replace everything underneath; Push The Feeling On (MK Dub '95, 122 Bm): resequence syllables into a meaningless riff; voice as drum machine; Music Sounds Better With You (Stardust '98, 124): filter cutoff IS the arrangement; one 2-bar loop + one line, hard pump.\n\n**Deep/afro/Innervisions**: Move (Adam Port/Stryv '24, 120 A\u266dm): sub-bass melody duets a falsetto phrase; snap not clap; drums whisper; Superman (Black Coffee '09, 124 \u2248A\u266d): complete soul song over a hand-percussion carpet, zero EDM build grammar; We Dance Again (Black Coffee '15, 122 \u2248F\u266fm): motion from one evolving arp, no drum fills; Muy\u00e8 (Keinemusik '17, 120 Am): build = adding perc layers, no white noise anywhere; Rej (\u00c2me '05, 125 \u2248C\u266fm): one modulating arp evolves 8+ minutes; one element changes at a time; patience.\n\n**Crossover/chart**: Latch (Disclosure '12, 122 \u2248Fm): jazz-grade harmony under a pop belt; falsetto leap on the title word; Ocean Drive (Duke Dumont '15, 115 G\u266fm): palette-first: the 80s era carries the emotion; Cola (CamelPhat '17, 122 \u2248F\u266f): restraint: holes in the bass on 2/4, talking-register vocal; Piece Of Your Heart (Meduza '19, 124 B\u266dm): sung phrase asks, pitched-chop melody answers = the drop; B.O.T.A. (Eliza Rose '22, 137 \u2248C/Am): joyful major piano + garage swing outside the tempo box; Padam Padam (Kylie '23, 128 \u2248Fm): invent a two-syllable percussive word; hook becomes the bass instrument; Houdini (Dua '23, 117 \u2248Bm): dance-pop below house tempo, PLAYED human pocket; Dracula (Tame Impala '25, 115 E\u266dm): menace from minor bass-8th chug, not sound design; No Broke Boys (Disco Lines/Tinashe '25, 131 \u2248C\u266fm): hook scans as SPEECH: chantable before singable; Nanana (Peggy Gou '23, 130 Gm): interpolate a shared memory (ATB pluck), answer with scat; Pjanoo (Prydz '08, 126 Gm): write the riff FIRST; if it needs a vocal to work, it isn't done.\n\n**Cross-patterns**: (1) tempo barbell: the 2018\u201320 canon holds 123\u2013126 but 2023\u201325 hits split: club crossovers 128\u2013137, pop-dance 115\u2013117; \"120\u2013128\" is the center, not the hits. (2) Hooks are borrowed or non-lexical: flips, interpolations, chants, non-words; hook stuck\u2192steal legally or go non-lexical. (3) Identity migrated into the bass register (90s = keys/vocal signature; 2018+ = talking bass/semitone riff/gallop/duet sub); element counts stayed 4\u20136 throughout. (4) Minor dominates; harmonic thinness is load-bearing (Losing It = 2 notes). (5) One mechanism per record: the filter, the chop, the pump, the silence gag; never two.\n\n---\n\n## 6. ARRANGEMENT + DROP MATH\n\n**Bar math** @124: 1 bar = 1.94 s, 8 = 15.5, 16 = 31, 32 = 62 (@120: 2.0/16/32/64; @128: 1.88/15/30/60). **Phrase discipline is the law**: built in 4\u21928\u219216\u219232; every entrance/exit/drop lands on an 8-bar boundary, almost always 16 or 32; DJs count 32-bar phrases; bar-13 changes are unmixable. The one non-negotiable rule.\n\n**Club/extended template** (5:30\u20137:00): DJ intro 16\u201332 (drums-led, near-harmonically-empty so keys can't clash in the blend; bassline waits)\u2192groove build 16\u201332\u2192breakdown 1 (8\u201316)\u2192build (8\u201316)\u2192drop 1 (32)\u2192mid 16\u201332\u2192main breakdown 16\u201332 (longest, most emotional)\u2192build 2 (biggest)\u2192drop 2 (+3) 32\u201364\u2192outro 16\u201332 mirroring the intro, clean end on a phrase downbeat. Skew: tech = short breaks, 3 drop cycles; deep = no hard drops, waves; diva = verse/pre/chorus with riff breaks; French = one loop + filter arc. **Radio edit** (3:05\u20134:00): \u22648-bar intro, hook by 0:15, two chorus cycles, one break. **Streaming edit** (2:45\u20133:20): hook inside 10 s, no DJ furniture, cold end (fade = skip-trigger). Ship BOTH same day + instrumental + acapella (the remix pack is the second promo wave); the extended earns DJ support. TikTok cut: 45\u201390 s loopable build+drop, often +10\u201320% tempo.\n\n**Energy curve**: first break \u22481/3 in (after ~two 32-bar phrases), main breakdown \u22482/3; never open with the longest break; breakdowns 8\u201316 bars (tech) to 16\u201332 (vocal). **Build anatomy** (8/16): snare roll doubling density every 4 bars (1/4\u21921/8\u21921/16\u21921/32); riser the whole build; hats and claps REMOVED (removal creates hunger); kick swells or exits the last 4; THE GAP: last 1\u20132 beats of near-silence holding only a vocal stab/reverse cymbal (beat prediction continues through silence: the drop lands on maximum prediction). **Drop confirmation**: bar 1 = kick + sub + signature hook + crash on the downbeat; drop 1 stays pure 16 bars. **Second-drop menu (pick 1\u20132)**: perc layer, kick to 8ths, countermelody or key lift, extend 16 bars, double the vocal. **Breathing**: strip something 4\u20138 bars every 32; drop the kick the last bar of a phrase; payoff every ~90 s; max two builds back-to-back; never two breakdowns without a full drop between; drop 2 weaker than drop 1 empties floors. Change SOMETHING every 16\u201332 bars forever. Lengths: 86\u201392 12\" 5:45\u20137:30; 90s radio 3:05\u20134:01; modern extended 5:30\u20136:45; modern single 2:45\u20134:10, shrinking.\n\n**Pop structure math**: intro 0\u20134 bars; verse 8/16; pre 4/8 (destabilize: leave I, start ii/IV/vi, end on V or DROPOUT; stepwise ascent; drums 8ths\u219216ths; bass out so chorus bass is an event); chorus 8/16, tonic-centered, title + peak density; post-chorus 4/8 (wordless hook, 2\u20133 notes); bridge 4\u20138 (breakdown or the quotable confession; never same energy AND same chords); outro cold. First full chorus \u22480:30\u20130:45 (Espresso 0:08 chorus-first). Templates: 2000s radio I4-V8-P8-C8-V8-P8-C8-B8-C\u00d72 (3:30\u20133:50); streaming (I0\u20132)-V8-P4\u20138-C8(+post4)-V8-P4-C8-B4\u20138-C+tag (2:35\u20133:15); TikTok-first C8-V8-P4-C8-post-V8-C-B-C (2:20\u20132:50). Two verses max; V2 never a copy: different subset + one new element. Key: chorus melody first, money note at C5\u2013E5 (female belt) / F4\u2013A4 (male), transpose: that IS the key; bass \u2265E1. Key changes: 23% of #1s 1958\u201390, ONE in the 2010s: now a deliberate spectacle; lift via double-time feel, stacks, or iv\u2192I. Color budget: voicings (add9/sus/maj7) free; ONE function-changing borrow per section (iv = heartbreak, bVII = swagger, bVI\u2013bVII\u2013I surge). Axis loops: I\u2013V\u2013vi\u2013IV earnest; vi\u2013IV\u2013I\u2013V melancholy engine (2010s default); IV\u2013I\u2013V\u2013vi floaty; start the loop on the chord whose color the section wears.\n\n**Topline & hook math**: melody first, lyrics serve phonetics (vowels beat meaning); paired lines mirror syllable counts; preview a chorus fragment in the verse; one glue-phrase across sections; 3\u20134 melodic parts max; highest note in the section's second half. ~70% of intervals are steps; after a leap \u2265P4, reverse and fill stepwise; leap TO stressed beats; start on 1/3/5; end on 1 to close, 2/5 to loop (house riffs end unresolved on purpose). One peak per phrase at ~60\u201375%; phrase-final long note (\u22652\u00d7 average) = the memory save-point. Contours: arch (chorus); axis chant (house topline); descent; ascent (pre); stairs. Earworm = maximally generic shape + exactly ONE anomaly; two twists = clever, and clever isn't catchy. Q\u2013A: phrase A ends on 2/5, A\u2032 repeats material, ends on 1. Chorus = the 15\u201330 s contagion unit. Title: in the chorus for 71% of hits (first/last line), sung ~6\u201312+ times; 1\u20133 open-vowel words; stressed syllables on strong beats, never articles; each extra chorus repetition \u2248 +14\u201315% odds of #1. Dose: pop hook 3\u00d7/chorus; house cell \u00d78\u201332 while the ARRANGEMENT moves. Run 3+ hook types at once. Writing order: rhythmic cell first (clap-able, \u22642 bars, one syncopation)\u2192pitch it (3\u20135 notes, \u2264P5 riff, start 1/3/5)\u2192one named contour\u2192ONE named twist\u2192Q\u2013A\u2192develop by transformation only (sequence/invert/augment/fragment; 1\u20132 motifs per song)\u2192dose repetition\u2192sing it, wait 4 hours, change the melody TO whatever you misremember\u2192prosody check. Not sticking\u2192twist count 0 or 3+; amateur\u2192unresolved leaps; loop grates by bar 16\u2192end note closed on 1, move to 2/5.\n\n**Virality card** (0\u20132 each, 24 max; 20+ = hit-capable, 14\u201319 = fix the two lowest, <14 = album track): unique 5-second fingerprint; hook by 0:30, chorus by 0:45; one designed 10\u201315 s clip moment (quotable / actable / hard pivot); catchphrase typeable after one listen (bonus if slightly \"wrong\"); length 2:15\u20133:15, ends hot, no outro; familiarity anchor + ONE nameable deviation; nameable emotional utility; rhythm encodes a repeatable action/POV; survives +25% tempo; title = hook = most-repeated phrase; the interesting thing NOT detachable from the artist; no dead 15-second window. Quality sets floor and ceiling; social proof decides the middle. Viral pipeline: DJ-set/crowd clip premiere (15\u201320 s, hook resolves inside the clip)\u2192template lock\u2192UGC\u2192streams lag 2\u20136 wks\u2192edit ladder (official sped-up/slowed, pop-feature remix).\n\n**Current meta (2026)**: organic over processed: real percussion with hand timing, warmth, real sung vocals, round pitched subs (log-drum DNA), garage swing inside 4/4, played basslines as hooks. Dates-you-to-2020: spoken \"party/drugs\" loops, static mid-saw, velocity-flat quantization, legacy risers + telegraphed drops, formant-chop as the only hook, extreme pad pump, slap-house bounce, trap hats under pop toplines, over-tuned mega-stacks. Waves: tech house = the establishment (124\u2013130); afro house = fastest riser (118\u2013124, warm subs, patience, no hard pump); amapiano tools leak everywhere (112\u2013116); UKG/speed garage 130\u2013140 re-entering; audible AI vocals = credibility damage in house right now. Nostalgia-flip house (known/PD vocal + 122\u2013126 house bed) is a proven working formula: the Sweet Caroline play.\n\n---\n\n## 7. MIX NUMBERS\n\n**Gain staging**: channels average \u2248\u221218 dBFS, mix bus peaks \u2264\u22126, master fader 0, trim with clip gain. House (kick = ref, peaks \u22128 to \u22126 dBFS): bass \u22123 to \u22125 (RMS \u2248 equal); clap \u22124 to \u22126; hats \u22128 to \u221214; lead/stab \u22126 to \u22129; pads \u221210 to \u221214; vocal \u22124 to \u22126; FX \u221210 to \u221216. Pop (lead vocal = ref): kick \u22122 to \u22124; snare \u22123 to \u22125; bass \u22124 to \u22126; BGVs/synths \u22126 to \u221210; hats \u221212 to \u221216; pads \u221214 to \u221218. Mute the reference: the mix should break.\n\n**EQ** (cuts narrow \u22643 dB, boosts wide \u22642 dB, subtract first): kick: cut 250\u2013350 box, boost 50\u201360 weight / 2\u20134k click, HP 25\u201330; bass: HP 25\u201330, \u22122 at kick's fundamental, +2\u20133 at 700\u2013900 (phone audibility); clap: HP 100\u2013120, +200 body, +3\u20135k crack; hats: kill lows to 200\u2013400, +8\u201310k shelf; pads/keys: HP 150\u2013250, cut 250\u2013350 mud, cut 2\u20135k away from vocal; piano: HP 180\u2013200; lead vocal: HP 80\u2013120, cut 300\u2013500 mud, +3\u20135k presence, +10k air; chops/FX: HP 250\u2013300. High-pass everything except kick and bass; cut where the meter stops moving. Masking: kick vs bass 40\u2013100 (sidechain + reciprocal carves); vocal vs synths (cut instruments \u22122 to \u22124 at 2\u20134k); hats vs air (shelf down). EQ Three = DJ tool: low-kill the sampled loop so your kick+bass own the bottom; automate GainLow \u2212inf\u21920 on the drop.\n\n**Compression** (set ratio/attack/release, then threshold to GR target): kick 4:1, 10\u201330 ms, 50\u2013100, 2\u20134 dB (often skip); bass 3\u20134:1, 5\u201315, 40\u201380, 3\u20135; clap 4:1, 2\u20133 dB; HATS NEVER; stabs 2\u20133:1, 1\u20133 dB; drum bus 2:1, 30 ms, auto, 1\u20132 glue; pop lead two stages: 3:1 10\u201320 ms 3\u20135 dB, then 4:1 1\u20135 ms 2\u20133 dB; BGVs 4:1, 4\u20136 dB (flatter tucks). Parallel drum smash: 8:1\u2013inf, 1 ms, 8\u201312 dB GR, blended 6\u201310 dB under. Parallel vocal: 6:1, 6\u20138 dB, \u2248\u221210 under. De-esser (no stock): Compressor sidechain-EQ bell 6\u20138 kHz, 4:1, rel 20\u201340, 2\u20134 dB on esses. No named problem (\"evenness/punch/glue\") = no compressor.\n\n**Sidechain matrix** (from kick, or a muted ghost-kick track for breakdown ducking): bass 4\u20137 dB, 0.1\u20131 ms / 150\u2013250 ms; pads/music bus 2\u20134 dB / 200\u2013300; French full-bus pump 6\u201310 dB / 300\u2013450; returns 3\u20136 dB; vocal bed 2\u20133 dB; hats NEVER. Pop: 1\u20132 dB inaudible default; dance chorus 3\u20135 dB; returns ducked 3\u20135 dB off the LEAD VOCAL (most of \"modern pop vocal\" is this one trick).\n\n**Space**: 2 returns: A \"room\": Reverb 1.6 s, pre-delay 20 ms, HP 250 / LP 6\u20137 kHz; B \"throw\": ping-pong dotted-1/8, fb 35%, HP 300 / LP 5k, ducked 2\u20133 dB. Sends: kick/bass/sub bone dry always; clap 15\u201320%; hats \u22645%; chords 10\u201315% (or Chorus-Ensemble); lead vocal 15\u201325%, pre-delay 25\u201330 ms + dotted-1/8 throw on phrase ends; chops small insert 0.6 s. Filter every return. Decays: drum room 0.4\u20130.8 s; clap 1.0\u20131.5; vocal plate 1.4\u20132.2; pad hall 2.5\u20134; FX bomb 4\u20138. Delays: vocal slap 80\u2013120 ms fb 0\u201310% (reads \"dry\"); throw 1/4 fb 25\u201340%; house groove dotted-1/8 fb 20\u201335%; dub 1/4\u20131/2 fb 50\u201370% narrow. Dry-vocal era: slap + tucked ducked 1/4 + short plate felt only in gaps; if you can HEAR reverb it's too loud. Depth: close = kick/bass/lead/clap near-dry; mid = stabs/keys/BGVs short verb; far = pads/FX long dark verb, quieter.\n\n**Stereo**: mono \u2264100\u2013120 Hz (Utility Bass Mono); kick/bass/lead/snare center; hats 110\u2013125% or panned \u00b110\u201330; stabs 100\u2013120; pads 120\u2013140 (the designated wide layer); BGVs 120\u2013140 or hard-panned; FX to 150; >200% = phase risk. Mono-check the master: nothing vanishes, low end drops <1 dB.\n\n**Saturation**: bass Soft Sine 3\u20136 dB (harmonics 100\u2013900 Hz = phone audibility); drum bus Analog Clip 2\u20134 dB; kick click Hard 3\u20136; vocal Medium 1\u20133 dB at 30\u201350% wet; master Soft Sine 1\u20132 dB soft-clip. Match output down by the drive. Lo-fi: Redux 12-bit/11\u201326 kHz = cassette/SP; Erosion Wide Noise 6\u20138 kHz = air grit.\n\n**Master chain**: Utility Bass Mono 100\u2013120\u2192Channel EQ \u00b11\u20132 dB max\u2192glue 2:1, 10\u201330 ms, auto, 1\u20132 dB\u2192Saturator Soft Sine 1\u20132 dB\u2192Limiter ceiling \u22121.0 dB, 2\u20134 dB GR loudest section (stock pumps past ~4\u20135 dB). Loudness: Spotify/YouTube \u221214 LUFS, Apple \u221216; master pop \u22129 to \u221211, house \u22128 to \u221210, \u2264\u22121.0 dBTP; club WAV \u22126 to \u22128 LUFS-I. Normalization only turns DOWN; <5 LU dynamics is what kills masters. One \u22128/\u221210 master covers streaming; render the hot club master separately.\n\n**Spectrum reference** (house, energy share): sub 20\u201360 Hz 15\u201322%; low 60\u2013120 18\u201325%; low-mid 120\u2013250 10\u201315%; mid 250\u2013500 8\u201312%; high-mid 0.5\u20132k 10\u201315%; presence 2\u20136k 8\u201312%; brilliance 6\u201312k 5\u20138%; air 2\u20134%. Shape: plateau 40\u2013100 Hz then \u22123 to \u22124.5 dB/oct tilt. Red flags: >30% sub; 250\u2013500 hump; presence louder than low. Crest 6\u20139 dB. Law: exactly ONE element competes with the kick: the hook; everything else \u22653 dB down and drier than you think.\n\n**Translation checks**: 80% of mixing at conversation volume; mono toggle; phone-sim (HP 400\u2013500 + LP 6\u20138k: ID the kick by click, follow the bass by 700\u2013900 harmonics?); real small speakers (phone = low-mids, earbuds = harshness, car = bass); reference A/B gain-matched (pro track needs ~\u22126 dB); the overnight low-volume listen beats the last 3 hours.\n\n**Diagnosis**: muddy\u2192cut 250\u2013350 on pads/keys/bass, HP non-bass \u2265120; harsh\u2192\u22122\u20133 at 3\u20134k non-vocal; saturate, don't brighten; thin\u2192lower HPs, +150\u2013250 on one element; kick no punch\u2192comp attack \u226510 ms, carve bass at kick fundamental, SC \u22654 dB; click no weight\u2192+50\u201360 Hz or sub layer; bass invisible on phone\u2192Soft Sine 4\u20136 dB + 700\u2013900; kick+bass mush\u2192sidechain + assign fundamentals; vocal buried\u2192cut competitors 2\u20134k, duck returns; vocal pasted-on\u2192slap 80\u2013120 ms + whisper of the band's verb; stiff groove\u2192un-compress hats, vel \u00b110\u201320, swing 55\u201358; collapses in mono\u2192width \u2264120, Bass Mono on; loud but small\u2192back the limiter 2\u20133 dB, automate sections \u00b11\u20132 dB; quieter than references at equal LUFS\u2192fill 700 Hz\u20133 kHz (perceived loudness lives there); can't hear anymore\u2192stop; verdicts tomorrow, low volume.\n\n**Vocal build**: stack = lead (ref) + 2 doubles hard L/R \u22126 to \u221212 + octave up \u221210 to \u221215 + harmony pairs (3rds/5ths, phrase-TAILS first) wide \u22128 to \u221212 + whisper unison \u221210 to \u221218 + octave down (menace) + ad-libs wetter/off-center. Verse = lead alone; pre = +1 harmony; chorus = full wall (8\u201316 tracks); bridge = strip; final = everything + ad-lib lane. Stack bus: 2:1, 2\u20133 dB, HP 150, LP 12k, half the lead's reverb. Tune doubles+harmonies HARDER than the lead (loose lead over tight stacks = human AND big). Ad-libs live in the lead's rests; echo last 2\u20133 words; escalate chorus 1 clean\u21922 tails\u2192final full. Chain: HPF\u2192fast comp (4:1, 1\u20133 ms, 3\u20136 dB)\u2192slow leveler (2:1, 2\u20134 dB)\u2192de-ess\u2192Saturator\u2192+10\u201312k air\u2192sends\u2192\u00b11\u20133 dB phrase rides (nothing replaces riding). Comping: 3\u20138 passes; emotion outranks pitch; keep breaths (\u22126 to \u221210 dB); comp lead FIRST, stack to the printed comp. AI vocals: breaths first (highest-value fix); sing the guide yourself and voice-convert; whisper-overdub the lyric under the AI lead (\u221210 to \u221215); regenerate every stack layer (never copy-paste clones); nudge phrase starts \u00b110\u201330 ms one direction; or COMMIT-as-instrument (hard-tune, chop, resample: a confident robot beats an uncanny human). PD recordings (Marion Harris): de-noise lightly, crackle is the charm; band-pass 200 Hz\u20134 kHz to own its radio frame, modern low end and air from the track; gentle tuning; CONTRAST layer, not intimacy-carrier.\n\n---\n\n## 8. DANCEABILITY CHECKLIST\n\nEvidence: medium syncopation + LOW/MED harmonic complexity rates highest (7ths/9ths in groove sections; chromatic weirdness only in breakdowns); the inverted-U STRENGTHENS with more rhythmic layers (a syncopated cell solo = noise, inside a kit = groove); quantized and performed-size microtiming tie: exaggerated looseness always loses; inaudible 8\u201337 Hz VLF bass = +11.8% audience movement (the sub does behavioral work you can't hear on laptops: trust the meter); louder kick = more movement; movement tracks pulse clarity, percussiveness, and LOW-FREQUENCY FLUX (a rest-punctuated moving bassline beats a drone); lab tempo optimum 107\u2013126 BPM (club 120\u2013128 at its top edge); drops produce the highest skin-conductance of any section and post-drop movement EXCEEDS pre-breakdown baseline: breakdowns are an investment with interest; repetition shifts listening from analysis to participation: counts that feel excessive in the chair are usually correct for the floor. Spotify danceability = beat-legibility only: <\u22480.6 flags a problem, 0.95 means nothing. DJ practice: key first (Camelot: same code, \u00b11, or A\u2194B), then BPM, then energy direction; every peak gets a 1\u20132-level breather; the big tune is spent at an established peak, not led with; floors are cleared by vibe mismatch, key clashes, sustained peaks, taste-over-function. Stats: tech house median 128 (124\u2192129\u2013130 over 2018\u201326); Beatport top-100 mean \u2248124.6; dominant key 8A (A minor), then 7B, 4A; \u224865% minor. Deliver in a populated Camelot neighborhood, mixable head/tail, UNAMBIGUOUS energy number.\n\n**The 12 falsifiable checks** (ship at 10/12; #1, #2, #4 mandatory: fail those and it is not a dance record yet): **1** tactus 1.7\u20132.1 Hz (felt beat ~102\u2013126 BPM, or clean half-time landing there); **2** pulse legible \u22641 bar: a stranger claps the beat within 4 counts; **3** medium syncopation, kit-framed: ONE named syncopator cell inside a multi-layer kit; **4** low end does behavioral work: clean 40\u201360 Hz, bassline with low-frequency flux, kick unapologetic; **5** harmony stays out of the way in groove sections; **6** microtiming at-or-below performed size, one polarity, kick anchored; **7** loudness breathes at beat rate on more than the bass; **8** break-routine grammar intact: withdrawal + intensification + gap before every drop; post-drop = densest movement; **9** scheduled novelty: change every 16, structural change every 32; each drop adds exactly one element; **10** energy level unambiguous 1\u201310; **11** DJ-deployable: \u226516-bar head/tail, populated Camelot key, tempo \u00b13 BPM of the genre median; **12** couch/floor test: core loop survived 20 minutes of passive listening, and at least one body that isn't yours moved unprompted.\n\n---\n\n## 9. PRODUCER CONDUCT\n\n**Method one-liners**: Rubin: subtract until only what carries emotion remains; be the song's first audience, feeling before analysis; \"does the song get worse without this?\" No\u2192cut. Quincy: 30 songs\u21929; remix a finished album if playback doesn't thrill; cast the right players then get out of the way; the body's reaction is the tiebreaker (Billie Jean's intro stayed because it made Michael dance). Max Martin: interrogate the melody for weeks BEFORE production; demo quality = final quality; never fix a weak melody with sound design. George Martin: never answer a weird request with \"can't\": answer with a mechanism for the feeling named. Rodgers: every song needs a Deep Hidden Meaning statable in one sentence; a song that isn't working gets its rhythmic chassis rewritten, not polish. Eno: when stuck, impose an arbitrary constraint and TRUST it; a mistake is information about a song you didn't know you were writing. Pharrell: the drums-plus-one-element loop must be undeniable naked. Timbaland: beatbox the groove before programming it; mutate one element per phrase. Dre: replay samples with players instead of clearing; record 10\u00d7 more than you keep; shipping is also a decision. Daft Punk: pick constraints on purpose and blame them for your style; capture human performance loosely, edit with machine precision. Fred again..: capture emotion the moment it happens; sketch in hours to preserve the trigger. Calvin Harris: cast every role, including yourself, by \"who's best.\"\n\n**Dance masters**: Summit: 30-minute sketch, then months of touring as A/B infrastructure; MacBook speakers till it sounds good; ear candy = LAST pass. Dolla: bassline melody as tight as possible; two parallel bass channels (pure sub + top grit); Erosion on basslines; club-tests before the label hears it. FISHER: three elements (bass hook, spoken phrase, drums), proven live for months; energy as a production value. Lake: mute-the-bass test: the loop must feel good BOTH ways (the breakdown IS the bass-out version); bounce to audio early, committing exposes problems; fast attack AND release sidechain. Mau P: keep the accident that scares you; three drops, each adds one element. Cloonee: chants are rhythm first. Kaytranada: kick DEAD on grid, hats late, snare early, constant; swing everything including chops; 108 BPM; sidechain nearly everything; chords split into two 3-note halves hard-panned. MK: chop vocals into syllables, replay as a riff, meaning secondary; EQ out inaudible junk; minimal compression; imperfect speakers. Disclosure: record the vocal faster in a higher key, slow to target (varispeed thickens); sidechain the WHOLE mix from a muted ghost trigger; kick from 4 spectrum-cast sources. PDM: most time on the bassline; drums + bass first. Duke Dumont: catalogue over trend. Black Coffee: session players are the magic touch; density lives in percussion layers, not the syncopation budget; tracks must work at minute 4 of 7. Keinemusik: detune EVERYTHING slightly + room tone under; the 20-MINUTE COUCH TEST: a loop that survives passive listening survives a club; sketch in 40\u2013120 min, ~20 versions, usually return to v1; done = feels right in the club, no screen. Solomun: strip to core, rebuild with a long runway. Dixon: modern tracks are too stuffed: REMOVE until they function; peaks are spent, then rebuilt; build removable layers + one huggable identity element. Knuckles: the dancefloor tells you the arrangement: extend what moves bodies, cut what doesn't. Todd Terry: change something every 16\u201332 bars. MAW: import swing by sampling players who have it. AVH: a filter on a great loop is a complete arrangement; recognizability is a feature.\n\n**The 10-rule make-them-dance manual**:\n1. Write the bassline hook FIRST; it must survive soloed with just kick+clap; spend more time here than anywhere.\n2. Body target picks the tempo: hips 105\u2013115, feet 120\u2013128, sway 115\u2013125 afro. Never inherit tempo from habit.\n3. Kick on the grid. ONE layer gets a constant push or pull of a few ms: pick a polarity, keep it all track.\n4. Cap the drop at ~5 sounding elements; adding one = muting one. The vocal counts.\n5. One short vocal phrase as a percussion instrument; chop it MK-style if longer than a bar.\n6. Sidechain more than the bass: pads/chords/sample too, gently; tight attack and release.\n7. Schedule novelty: something changes every 16, something REAL every 32; each drop adds exactly one element.\n8. Couch test: loop the core 20 minutes while doing something else. Boredom before minute 20 = fix the loop, not the arrangement.\n9. Bounce to audio and commit early; check on the smallest speaker you own.\n10. Before release, play it for bodies and believe their feet over their words.\n\n**Mid-session rules**: play the current state once through, write the first feeling in one sentence: it outranks later analysis; bones first: melody+chords+drums must excite on plain sounds; loop test: the core 4\u20138 bars soloed must survive 16 plays; know the DHM; over-generate 3+ options, pick by instant gut; mute two before adding one; mutate one groove element per 8 bars; audition every accident 4\u00d7 before undoing; stuck >15 min\u2192arbitrary constraint, trust it one pass; not best-in-room after 3 attempts\u2192replace the source, don't process the failure; done-ness = body test at volume; fresh-ears checkpoint every 2 hours; one decider; ship on the deadline. Element framework, first NO wins: Necessary? (mute it: lose feeling, not just fullness?)\u2192Right source?\u2192Serving the DHM?\u2192Pocket-true?\u2192Earning its space?\u2192Done? Two song modes, decided once, early: feel-songs die from revision; craft-songs die from haste. Failure modes: polishing before the bones work; additive panic; chasing 18-month-old trends; committee averaging; perfection-as-avoidance; quantizing away the wobble that made the take. Name what's working, then the ONE fix, framed as an experiment; the first comment after a silent full-volume playback is the truest data of the day.\n\n**Brady-session conduct**: music is ALWAYS playing: edit live, never present a finished thing; new-sound ritual: pause, play the candidate alone, \"in or out?\", on IN put it in and iterate by voice. Taste uncertain\u2192render BOTH, \"A or B?\": the primary taste-extraction tool; never describe options you can play. A repeated complaint = the previous fix FAILED: root-cause, never re-apply the same category of fix. Replay the smallest section that proves the change (4\u20138 bars). Disagree ONCE with the mechanism, then execute his call. Verbal sketches (\"bing bing bong\"): count = notes, vowel inflection = contour, pacing = rhythm; render his sketch FIRST. Say what to LISTEN FOR, not what you did. One question per turn, concrete either/or. His silence = the change passed.\n\n---\n\n## 10. BRADY'S TASTE + LEXICON (verbatim)\n\n### taste.md\n\n**HATES (strong, evidenced)**\n- Pitched-up chipmunk/screech vocal artifacts \u2014 \"that high pitch shit screech is not it\", \"kids bop\" (sweet-caroline sessions, flagged 3x before root-caused: sample played +16st above root)\n- Robot/TTS vocals \u2014 \"too robotic\", \"the voice is not smooth\" (killed all TTS clips 2026-08-24)\n- Rigid/quantized-feeling grooves \u2014 \"too rigid, too connected\", \"so perfect... music has mistakes\"\n- Everything-on-the-grid sameness; loops that never vary\n- Em/en dashes in any user-facing copy (global rule, applies to any text output)\n\n**LOVES (strong, evidenced)**\n- The After Glow \"Break Pads\" sound \u2014 \"I really really like the brake pads\" (but NOT under verse 2; high notes of it = good)\n- Real human voices with character \u2014 approved the Marion Harris 1919 blues placement direction\n- Rolling/moving bass, groove-first everything \u2014 \"I want this song to be more groovy than anything\"\n- Hook \u2192 beat-drop structure; big drops; crowd-psychology moves (silence gap validated)\n- Systems that learn and improve; being asked for his read via listening, not text walls\n\n**WORKING STYLE**\n- Ship fast, action over discussion; real constraint said ONCE then execute; never re-litigate.\n- He gives feedback in feel-words, often late at night, sometimes via voice dictation (typos \u2260 carelessness).\n- When he repeats a complaint, the previous fix failed \u2014 escalate to root cause.\n\n**OPEN QUESTIONS (ask via A/B when natural)**\n- Preferred drop density: rolling-tech-house (Dom Dolla) vs big-room?\n- Vintage/crackle vocal aesthetic vs clean modern topline for the final version?\n- How much swing is \"groovy\" TO HIM (55% vs 58%)? Render A/B once.\n\n### lexicon.md\n\n- \"screech\" / \"high pitch shit\" = sample pitched far above its natural register (chipmunk artifacts)\n- \"kidsey / Kids Bop\" = bright + quantized + pitched-up + toy-like preset quality\n- \"groovy\" = syncopated moving bass + pocket + dynamics breathing (NOT more layers)\n- \"rigid\" = no syncopation, no dynamic wave, loop never varies (NOT a request for random jitter)\n- \"hits / slaps\" = drop contrast + low-end weight + impact stack landed\n- \"robotic\" (vocal) = no pitch contour, no breath, no timing looseness; TTS-sounding\n- \"smooth\" (transitions) = filtered/riser-eased entries and exits, no hard cold cuts\n- \"cooking / keep cooking\" = proceed autonomously, batch multiple improvements\n- \"more of a sound\" = fuller arrangement density/width \u2014 verify via A/B, still calibrating\n\n\n# BRADY'S TASTE (living file \u2014 update every session with evidence)\n\n## HATES (strong, evidenced)\n- Pitched-up chipmunk/screech vocal artifacts \u2014 \"that high pitch shit screech is not it\",\n  \"kids bop\" (sweet-caroline sessions, flagged 3x before root-caused: sample played +16st above root)\n- Robot/TTS vocals \u2014 \"too robotic\", \"the voice is not smooth\" (killed all TTS clips 2026-08-24)\n- Rigid/quantized-feeling grooves \u2014 \"too rigid, too connected\", \"so perfect... music has mistakes\"\n- Everything-on-the-grid sameness; loops that never vary\n- Em/en dashes in any user-facing copy (global rule, applies to any text output)\n\n## LOVES (strong, evidenced)\n- The After Glow \"Break Pads\" sound \u2014 \"I really really like the brake pads\" (but NOT under verse 2;\n  high notes of it = good)\n- Real human voices with character \u2014 approved the Marion Harris 1919 blues placement direction\n- Rolling/moving bass, groove-first everything \u2014 \"I want this song to be more groovy than anything\"\n- Hook \u2192 beat-drop structure; big drops; crowd-psychology moves (silence gap validated)\n- Systems that learn and improve; being asked for his read via listening, not text walls\n\n## WORKING STYLE\n- Ship fast, action over discussion; real constraint said ONCE then execute; never re-litigate.\n- He gives feedback in feel-words, often late at night, sometimes via voice dictation (typos \u2260 carelessness).\n- When he repeats a complaint, the previous fix failed \u2014 escalate to root cause.\n\n## OPEN QUESTIONS (ask via A/B when natural)\n- Preferred drop density: rolling-tech-house (Dom Dolla) vs big-room?\n- Vintage/crackle vocal aesthetic vs clean modern topline for the final version?\n- How much swing is \"groovy\" TO HIM (55% vs 58%)? Render A/B once.\n";
